.SH "NAME"
pcre2compat \- PCRE2 compatibility
.SH "DIFFERENCES BETWEEN PCRE2 AND PERL"
.sp
This document describes some of the known differences in the ways that PCRE2
and Perl handle regular expressions. The differences described here are with
respect to Perl version 5.38.0, but as both Perl and PCRE2 are continually
changing, the information may at times be out of date.
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
When \fBPCRE2_DOTALL\fP (equivalent to Perl\(cqs \f(CR/s\fP qualifier) is not set, the
behaviour of the \f(CR.\fP metacharacter differs from Perl. In PCRE2, \f(CR.\fP matches the
next character unless it is the start of a newline sequence. This means that,
if the newline setting is CR, CRLF, or NUL, \f(CR.\fP will match the code point LF
(\f(CR0x0A\fP) in ASCII/Unicode environments, and NL (either \f(CR0x15\fP or \f(CR0x25\fP) when using
EBCDIC. In Perl, \f(CR.\fP appears never to match LF, even when \f(CR0x0A\fP is not a newline
indicator.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
PCRE2 has only a subset of Perl\(cqs Unicode support. Details of what it does
have are given in the
\f(CRpcre2unicode\fP
page.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Like Perl, PCRE2 allows repeat quantifiers on parenthesized assertions, but
they do not mean what you might think. For example, \f(CR(?!a){3}\fP does not assert
that the next three characters are not \f(CRa\fP. It just asserts that the next
character is not \f(CRa\fP three times (in principle; PCRE2 optimizes this to run the
assertion just once). Perl allows some repeat quantifiers on other assertions,
for example, \f(CR\(rsb*\fP , but these do not seem to have any use. PCRE2 does not allow
any kind of quantifier on non\-lookaround assertions.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
If a braced quantifier such as \f(CR{1,2}\fP appears where there is nothing to
repeat (for example, at the start of a branch), PCRE2 raises an error whereas
Perl treats the quantifier characters as literal. When a braced quantifier
\f(CR(...){\fP\fImin\fP\f(CR,\fP\fImax\fP\f(CR}\fP has \fImin\fP > \fImax\fP, Perl treats it as an item which fails to match
any portion of the subject (as no number of repetitions can meet the
condition), and additionally issues a warning when in warning mode. PCRE2 has
no warning features, so it gives an error in this case.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Capture groups that occur inside negative lookaround assertions are counted,
but their entries in the offsets vector are set only when a negative assertion
is a condition that has a matching branch (that is, the condition is false).
Perl may set such capture groups in other circumstances.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
The following Perl escape sequences are not supported: \f(CR\(rsF\fP, \f(CR\(rsl\fP, \f(CR\(rsL\fP, \f(CR\(rsu\fP,
\f(CR\(rsU\fP, and \f(CR\(rsN\fP when followed by a character name. \f(CR\(rsN\fP on its own, matching a
non\-newline character, and \f(CR\(rsN{U+\fP\fIdd...\fP\f(CR}\fP, matching a Unicode code point, are
supported. The escapes that modify the case of following letters are
implemented by Perl\(cqs general string\-handling and are not part of its pattern
matching engine. If any of these are encountered by PCRE2, an error is
generated by default. However, if either of the \fBPCRE2_ALT_BSUX\fP or
\fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ALT_BSUX\fP options is set, \f(CR\(rsU\fP and \f(CR\(rsu\fP are interpreted as ECMAScript
interprets them.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
The Perl escape sequences \f(CR\(rsp\fP, \f(CR\(rsP\fP, and \f(CR\(rsX\fP are supported only if PCRE2 is
built with Unicode support (the default). The properties that can be tested
with \f(CR\(rsp\fP and \f(CR\(rsP\fP are limited to the general category properties such as
\f(CRLu\fP and \f(CRNd\fP, the derived properties \f(CRAny\fP and \f(CRLc\fP
(synonym \f(CRL&\fP), script names such as \f(CRGreek\fP or \f(CRHan\fP,
\f(CRBidi_Class\fP, \f(CRBidi_Control\fP, and a few binary properties. Both PCRE2 and
Perl support the \f(CRCs\fP (surrogate) property, but in PCRE2 its use is limited. See
the
\f(CRpcre2pattern\fP
documentation for details. The long synonyms for property names that Perl
supports (such as \f(CR\(rsp{Letter}\fP) are not supported by PCRE2, nor is it permitted
to prefix any of these properties with \f(CRIs\fP.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
PCRE2 supports the \f(CR\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP escape for quoting substrings. Characters
in between are treated as literals. However, this is slightly different from
Perl in that \f(CR$\fP and \f(CR@\fP are also handled as literals inside the quotes. In Perl,
they cause variable interpolation (PCRE2 does not have variables). Also, Perl
does "double\-quotish backslash interpolation" on any backslashes between \f(CR\(rsQ\fP
and \f(CR\(rsE\fP which, its documentation says, "may lead to confusing results". PCRE2
treats a backslash between \f(CR\(rsQ\fP and \f(CR\(rsE\fP just like any other character. Note the
following examples:
