T}
.TE
.sp
.sp
The \f(CR\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP sequence is recognized both inside and outside character classes
by both PCRE2 and Perl. Another difference from Perl is that any appearance of
\f(CR\(rsQ\fP or \f(CR\(rsE\fP inside what might otherwise be a quantifier causes PCRE2 not to
recognize the sequence as a quantifier. Perl recognizes a quantifier if
(redundantly) either of the numbers is inside \f(CR\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP, but not if the
separating comma is. When not recognized as a quantifier a sequence such as
\f(CR{\(rsQ1\(rsE,2}\fP is treated as the literal string \f(CR{1,2}\fP.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Fairly obviously, PCRE2 does not support the \f(CR(?{\fP\fIcode\fP\f(CR})\fP and
\f(CR(??{\fP\fIcode\fP\f(CR})\fP
constructions. However, PCRE2 does have a "callout" feature, which allows an
external function to be called during pattern matching. See the
\f(CRpcre2callout\fP
documentation for details.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Subroutine calls (whether recursive or not) were treated as atomic groups
up to PCRE2 release 10.23, but from release 10.30 this changed, and
backtracking into subroutine calls is now supported, as in Perl.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
In PCRE2, if any of the backtracking control verbs are used in a group that
is called as a subroutine (whether or not recursively), their effect is
confined to that group; it does not extend to the surrounding pattern. This is
not always the case in Perl. In particular, if \f(CR(*THEN)\fP is present in a group
that is called as a subroutine, its action is limited to that group, even if
the group does not contain any \f(CR|\fP characters. Note that such groups are
processed as anchored at the point where they are tested. PCRE2 also confines
all control verbs within atomic assertions, again including \f(CR(*THEN)\fP in
assertions with only one branch.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
If a pattern contains more than one backtracking control verb, the first
one that is backtracked onto acts. For example, in the pattern
\f(CRA(*COMMIT)B(*PRUNE)C\fP a failure in \f(CRB\fP triggers \f(CR(*COMMIT)\fP, but a failure in \f(CRC\fP
triggers \f(CR(*PRUNE)\fP. Perl\(cqs behaviour is more complex; in many cases it is the
same as PCRE2, but there are cases where it differs.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
There are some differences that are concerned with the settings of captured
strings when part of a pattern is repeated. For example, matching \f(CRaba\fP against
the pattern \f(CR^(a(b)?)+$\fP in Perl leaves \f(CR$2\fP unset, but in PCRE2 it is set to
\f(CRb\fP.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
PCRE2\(cqs handling of duplicate capture group numbers and names is not as
general as Perl\(cqs. This is a consequence of the fact the PCRE2 works internally
just with numbers, using an external table to translate between numbers and
names. In particular, a pattern such as \f(CR(?|(?<a>A)|(?<b>B))\fP, where the two
capture groups have the same number but different names, is not supported, and
causes an error at compile time. If it were allowed, it would not be possible
to distinguish which group matched, because both names map to capture group
number 1. To avoid this confusing situation, an error is given at compile time.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Perl used to recognize comments in some places that PCRE2 does not, for
example, between the \f(CR(\fP and \f(CR?\fP at the start of a group. If the \f(CR/x\fP modifier is
set, Perl allowed white space between \f(CR(\fP and \f(CR?\fP though the latest Perls give an
error (for a while it was just deprecated). There may still be some cases where
Perl behaves differently.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Perl, when in warning mode, gives warnings for character classes such as
\f(CR[A\-\(rsd]\fP or \f(CR[a\-[:digit:]]\fP. It then treats the hyphens as literals. PCRE2 has no
warning features, so it gives an error in these cases because they are almost
certainly user mistakes.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
In PCRE2, until release 10.45, the upper/lower case character properties
\f(CRLu\fP and \f(CRLl\fP were not affected when case\-independent matching was specified. Perl has
changed in this respect, and PCRE2 has now changed to match. When caseless
matching is in force, \f(CRLu\fP, \f(CRLl\fP, and \f(CRLt\fP (title case)
are all treated as \f(CRLc\fP (cased letter).
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
From release 5.32.0, Perl locks out the use of \f(CR\(rsK\fP in lookaround
assertions. From release 10.38 PCRE2 does the same by default. However, there
is an option for re\-enabling the previous behaviour. When this option is set,
\f(CR\(rsK\fP is acted on when it occurs in positive assertions, but is ignored in
negative assertions.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
PCRE2 provides some extensions to the Perl regular expression facilities.
Perl 5.10 included new features that were not in earlier versions of Perl, some
of which (such as named parentheses) were in PCRE2 for some time before. This
list is with respect to Perl 5.38:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' a.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " a." 4.2
.\}
If \fBPCRE2_DOLLAR_ENDONLY\fP is set and \fBPCRE2_MULTILINE\fP is not set, the \f(CR$\fP
meta\-character matches only at the very end of the string.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' b.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " b." 4.2
.\}
A backslash followed by a letter with no special meaning is faulted. (Perl
can be made to issue a warning.)
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' c.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " c." 4.2
.\}
If \fBPCRE2_UNGREEDY\fP is set, the greediness of the repetition quantifiers is
inverted, that is, by default they are not greedy, but if followed by a
question mark they are.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' d.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " d." 4.2
.\}
\fBPCRE2_ANCHORED\fP can be used at matching time to force a pattern to be tried
only at the first matching position in the subject string.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' e.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " e." 4.2
.\}
The \fBPCRE2_NOTBOL\fP, \fBPCRE2_NOTEOL\fP, \fBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY\fP and \fBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY_ATSTART\fP
options have no Perl equivalents.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' f.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " f." 4.2
.\}
The \f(CR\(rsR\fP escape sequence can be restricted to match only CR,
LF, or CRLF
by the \fBPCRE2_BSR_ANYCRLF\fP option.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' g.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " g." 4.2
.\}
The callout facility is PCRE2\-specific. Perl supports codeblocks and
variable interpolation, but not general hooks on every match.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' h.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " h." 4.2
.\}
The partial matching facility is PCRE2\-specific.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' i.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " i." 4.2
.\}
The alternative matching function (pcre2_dfa_match()) matches in a
different way and is not Perl\-compatible.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' j.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " j." 4.2
.\}
PCRE2 recognizes some special sequences such as \f(CR(*CR)\fP or \f(CR(*NO_JIT)\fP at
the start of a pattern. These set overall options that cannot be changed within
the pattern.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' k.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " k." 4.2
.\}
PCRE2 supports non\-atomic positive lookaround assertions. This is an
extension to the lookaround facilities. The default, Perl\-compatible
lookarounds are atomic.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' l.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " l." 4.2
.\}
There are three syntactical items in patterns that can refer to a capturing
group by number: back references such as \f(CR\(rsg{2}\fP, subroutine calls such as \f(CR(?3)\fP,
and condition references such as \f(CR(?(4)...)\fP. PCRE2 supports relative group
numbers such as \f(CR+2\fP and \f(CR\-4\fP in all three cases. Perl supports both plus and minus
for subroutine calls, but only minus for back references, and no relative
numbering at all for conditions.
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' m.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " m." 4.2
.\}
The scan substring assertion (syntax \f(CR(*scs:(\fP\fIn\fP\f(CR)...)\fP) is a PCRE2 extension
that is not available in Perl.
.RE
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Perl has different limits than PCRE2. See the
\f(CRpcre2limits\fP
documentation for details. Perl went with 5.10 from recursion to iteration
keeping the intermediate matches on the heap, which is ~10% slower but does not
fall into any stack\-overflow limit. PCRE2 made a similar change at release
10.30, and also has many build\-time and run\-time customizable limits.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Unlike Perl, PCRE2 doesn\(cqt have character set modifiers and specially no way
to set characters by context just like Perl\(cqs \f(CR/d\fP. A regular expression using
\fBPCRE2_UTF\fP and \fBPCRE2_UCP\fP will use similar rules to Perl\(cqs \f(CR/u\fP; something closer
to \f(CR/a\fP could be selected by adding other \f(CRPCRE2_EXTRA_ASCII*\fP options on top.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Some recursive patterns that Perl diagnoses as infinite recursions can be
handled by PCRE2, either by the interpreter or the JIT. An example is
\f(CR(?:|(?0)abcd)(?(R)|\(rsz)\fP, which matches a sequence of any number of repeated
\f(CRabcd\fP substrings at the end of the subject.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Both PCRE2 and Perl error when \f(CR\(rsx{\fP escapes are invalid, but Perl tries to
recover and prints a warning if the problem was that an invalid hexadecimal
digit was found. Since PCRE2 doesn\(cqt have warnings it returns an error instead.
Additionally, Perl accepts \f(CR\(rsx{}\fP and matches NUL unlike PCRE2.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
From release 10.45, PCRE2 gives an error if \f(CR\(rsx\fP is not followed by a
hexadecimal digit or a curly bracket. It used to interpret this as the NUL
character. Perl still matches NUL, but warns when in warning mode in most
cases.
.RE
